The Gaussian integral $I_0$ is positive and finite: its integrand is positive and, for $r\ge1$, $e^{-r^2}\le e^{-r}$, whose tail is integrable. Differentiation gives $E'(s)=-e^{-s^2}/I_0$ and $E''(s)=-2sE'(s)$. For $s=y/(2\sqrt{
u t})$, $s_t=-s/(2t)$ and $s_y=1/(2\sqrt{
u t})$, so $u_t=-UsE'(s)/(2t)$ and $
u u_{yy}=-UsE'(s)/(2t)$. Thus $u_t=
u u_{yy}$; the field has zero divergence and zero convection because it has only an $x$ component and is independent of $x$. The pressure gradient is zero, so [F1] gives the exact equation. Also $E(0)=1$, $E(s)	o0$ as $s	o\infty$, and for fixed $y>0$, $s	o\infty$ as $t\downarrow0$. [given, F1, A1, algebra]

